# Line-ending policy.
#
# Every text file is stored with LF in the repository and checked out with LF on
# every platform. This pins what is already true for almost every tracked text
# file, so nothing has to be rewritten and this commit churns no line endings.
# The exception is two sample datasets committed with CRLF
# (sample_data/Life Expectancy Data.csv, sample_data/production_lots.csv): they
# are data rather than source, are read by pandas, and are left alone here. They
# will flip to LF the first time they are edited and re-added — a whole-file diff
# at that moment, not a correctness problem. Renormalising them deliberately is a
# separate change.
#
# It also settles an ambiguity: the developer machine sets `core.autocrlf=true`,
# which checks text out as CRLF and makes Git warn "LF will be replaced by CRLF
# the next time Git touches it" on every edit, while other clones of this repo
# would not. An attribute in the repository beats a setting in one person's
# config, so the working tree stops depending on who cloned it.
#
# LF is safe here because nothing tracked needs CRLF: no .bat/.cmd (whose parser
# is line-ending sensitive) and no .ps1 are in the repository at all.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The shipped artifacts are zip archives, not text. `text=auto` would detect them
# as binary on its own, but a misdetection would rewrite an .xlsx as text and
# corrupt it silently, so the intent is stated rather than assumed.
*.xlsx binary
*.pptx binary
